   "source": [
    "### Maximum Log-Likelihood\n",
    "For a Gaussian random variable, the (normalized) log-likelihood is:\n",
    "$$-\\frac{1}{2} \\log(2\\pi) - \\frac{1}{2}\\log(\\sigma^2) - \\frac{1}{2 N \\sigma^2}\\sum_{n=1}^{N} (x_i-\\mu)^2$$\n",
    "It was shown in class that this is maximized when $\\mu$ is the mean of the data, $\\{x_i\\}$, and $\\sigma^2$ its variance. We shall show this empirically with our BMI data. We will do this with our traning data"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 8,
   "id": "a3bb60a1-ce1c-404c-9ace-8bc09a8079a0",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Train log-likelihood at MLE: 1.611602109399172\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "def gaussian_log_likelihood(x, mu, var):\n",
    "    \"\"\"Return total log-likelihood for data x under N(mu, var).\n",
    "    var must be positive (scalar).\"\"\"\n",
    "    n = x.size\n",
    "    # Use the closed-form expression\n",
    "    ll = -0.5 *  np.log(2 * np.pi)\n",
    "    ll += -0.5 *  np.log(var)\n",
    "    ll += -0.5 * np.sum((x - mu) ** 2) / (var*n)\n",
    "    return ll\n",
    "\n",
    "# Quick check: compute log-likelihood at MLE\n",
    "ll_mle = gaussian_log_likelihood(X_train, mu_mle, var_mle)\n",
    "print('Train log-likelihood at MLE:', ll_mle)\n"
   ]
